Description: CVE-2026-40691 - Packet of death for DNSCrypt over TCP
 A specially crafted DNSCrypt query received over TCP could cause unbound to
 write past the bounds of the DNSCrypt reply buffer while curving (encrypting)
 the response, leading to a denial of service (crash) or memory corruption.
 Over TCP the advertised reply size was set to 65535, but the DNSCrypt reply
 buffer capacity is smaller once the DNSCrypt reply header is accounted for.
 .
 This backport, adapted from upstream commit
 f54e0791ba4488284d9f6059014640a3d0bd30da for the 1.13.1 tree, hardens
 dnscrypt_server_curve() so it refuses to encrypt when the plaintext plus the
 DNSCrypt reply header would not fit in the buffer, and clamps the maximum
 reply length to the actual buffer capacity (and to 65535). The reply buffer
 is cleared/flipped correctly before writing the curved answer. The advertised
 EDNS udp_size for non-UDP (TCP) DNSCrypt clients is capped to the DNSCrypt
 buffer capacity minus the reply header, and dnsc_handle_uncurved_request()
 now takes the source buffer explicitly so the correct buffer (the TCP spool
 buffer when present) is copied.
 .
 The upstream testdata/dnscrypt_cert*.tdir test changes are not included:
 they assert a SERVFAIL cleartext response introduced in a later release,
 whereas the 1.13.1 cleartext path drops the reply. The existing shipped
 tdir tests already cover the unchanged drop-reply behavior.
Origin: upstream, https://github.com/NLnetLabs/unbound/commit/f54e0791ba4488284d9f6059014640a3d0bd30da
Author: W.C.A. Wijngaards <wouter@nlnetlabs.nl>
Last-Update: 2026-08-18
