Description: CVE-2026-32665 - not applicable to unbound 1.13.1 (Debian 11 bullseye)
 CVE-2026-32665 is a remote DNS-over-QUIC (DoQ) denial of service caused by a
 quic-size budget bypass. The upstream fix (commit 01dfd2f) modifies the DoQ
 stream-handling functions in services/listen_dnsport.c (doq_stream_open_cb,
 doq_stream_datalen_complete, doq_stream_pickup_answer, doq_stream_recv_data).
 .
 The entire DNS-over-QUIC subsystem was introduced in unbound 1.22.0, roughly
 3.5 years and nine minor releases after 1.13.1 (the version shipped in Debian
 11 "bullseye"). In the 1.13.1 source tree none of the affected code exists:
 services/listen_dnsport.c has no doq_stream_* functions, there is no
 ngtcp2/QUIC transport code, and configure.ac has no DoQ build support. The
 vulnerable code path therefore cannot be reached and the upstream fix can be
 neither applied nor back-ported.
 .
 This is a no-op documentation patch: it records the NOT-AFFECTED analysis and
 makes no code change, because no code change is required or possible for this
 version.
Origin: upstream, https://github.com/NLnetLabs/unbound/commit/01dfd2f466d383370405d8ecf939570947f3523e
Author: Aikido Security <security@aikido.dev>
Last-Update: 2026-08-18
Forwarded: not-needed
---
--- /dev/null
+++ b/doc/CVE-2026-32665.NOT-AFFECTED
@@ -0,0 +1,37 @@
+CVE-2026-32665 — Not applicable to unbound 1.13.1 (Debian 11 "bullseye")
+========================================================================
+
+Summary
+-------
+CVE-2026-32665 is a remote DNS-over-QUIC (DoQ) denial-of-service caused by a
+`quic-size` budget bypass. The upstream fix (NLnetLabs/unbound commit
+01dfd2f466d383370405d8ecf939570947f3523e) modifies the DoQ stream-handling
+code in services/listen_dnsport.c:
+
+  - doq_stream_open_cb()        : removes the unconditional budget bypass for
+                                  stream_id 0 and 4
+  - doq_stream_datalen_complete(): adds a quic-size availability check
+  - doq_stream_pickup_answer()  : adds a quic-size availability check
+  - doq_stream_recv_data()      : threads the doq_conn pointer through
+
+Why this version is not affected
+--------------------------------
+The entire DNS-over-QUIC subsystem was introduced in unbound 1.22.0, roughly
+3.5 years and nine minor releases after 1.13.1 (released February 2021, the
+version shipped in Debian 11 "bullseye" as 1.13.1-1+deb11u7).
+
+In this source tree (release-1.13.1) none of the affected code exists:
+
+  * services/listen_dnsport.c contains no doq_stream_* functions.
+  * There is no ngtcp2 / QUIC transport code anywhere in the tree.
+  * configure.ac has no --with-libngtcp2 option and no DoQ build support.
+
+Because there is no DNS-over-QUIC listener and no `quic-size` budget in this
+version, the vulnerable code path cannot be reached. The vulnerability does
+not apply to unbound 1.13.1.
+
+Conclusion
+----------
+No code change is required or possible for this version: the upstream fix
+touches functions that do not exist here, so it can be neither applied nor
+back-ported. This package version is NOT AFFECTED by CVE-2026-32665.
Description: Fix CVE-2026-33278 - use-after-free / RCE during DNSSEC validation.
 Upstream CVE-2026-33278 is a use-after-free in dns_msg_deepcopy_region()
 reached through the NSEC3 computational-budget suspend/resume path, both of
 which were introduced in unbound 1.19.1 and do not exist in this 1.13.1
 target. This backports the portable, applicable half of upstream fix commit
 6a31e470f80a6e5c559ebe7fd4cfc0582d3b6d0a: a defensive param_set_same() input
 check that rejects NSEC3 record sets whose members come from distinct NSEC3
 chains (mismatched algorithm, iteration count, or salt) or that are malformed,
 before running the expensive NSEC3 proofs. This mixed-chain/malformed NSEC3
 record set from a malicious signed zone is the attacker-controlled precondition
 that drives the excessive NSEC3 computation underlying the vulnerability. The
 guard is added at the entry of all five NSEC3 proof functions.
Origin: ai-synthesized, CVE-2026-33278
Author: Root.io Novel Patcher <security@root.io>
Last-Update: 2026-08-18

--- a/validator/val_nsec3.c
+++ b/validator/val_nsec3.c
@@ -443,6 +443,67 @@ filter_init(struct nsec3_filter* filter, struct ub_packed_rrset_key** list,
 	}
 }
 
+/** Check if the NSEC3s have the same parameter set. */
+static int
+param_set_same(struct nsec3_filter* flt, char** reason)
+{
+	size_t rrsetnum;
+	int rrnum;
+	struct ub_packed_rrset_key* rrset;
+	int have_params = 0;
+	int first_algo = 0;
+	size_t first_iter = 0;
+	uint8_t* first_salt = NULL;
+	size_t first_saltlen = 0;
+
+	/* If the NSEC3 parameter sets have distinct values, then they are
+	 * from different NSEC3 chains, and we do not want that. */
+	for(rrset=filter_first(flt, &rrsetnum, &rrnum); rrset;
+		rrset=filter_next(flt, &rrsetnum, &rrnum)) {
+		if(!have_params) {
+			first_algo = nsec3_get_algo(rrset, rrnum);
+			first_iter = nsec3_get_iter(rrset, rrnum);
+			if(!nsec3_get_salt(rrset, rrnum, &first_salt,
+				&first_saltlen)) {
+				verbose(VERB_ALGO, "NSEC3 salt malformed");
+				if(reason)
+					*reason = "NSEC3 salt malformed";
+				return 0;
+			}
+			have_params = 1;
+		} else {
+			uint8_t* salt = NULL;
+			size_t saltlen = 0;
+			if(nsec3_get_algo(rrset, rrnum) != first_algo) {
+				verbose(VERB_ALGO, "NSEC3 algorithm mismatch");
+				if(reason)
+					*reason = "NSEC3 algorithm mismatch";
+				return 0;
+			}
