Description: CVE-2026-40622 - Fix "Ghost domain name" variant
 Ghost-domain attacks let an attacker keep a revoked/expiring domain name
 resolvable indefinitely by repeatedly re-injecting NS RRsets whose TTL is
 larger than the one already cached. When a more-trustworthy NS RRset is
 stored (newd->trust > cached->trust) unbound accepted the attacker-supplied
 TTL verbatim, extending the lifetime of the delegation past the entry it
 replaces. This clamps the new NS RRset TTL (and its per-RR TTLs) to that of
 the non-expired cached entry it overwrites, so an NS refresh can never
 extend a delegation's lifetime.
 .
 The upstream fix uses the TTL_IS_EXPIRED() macro, which is not present in
 this version; it is hand-translated here to the equivalent raw ttl vs
 timenow comparison already used by the sibling same-trust NS clamp in this
 function.
Origin: upstream, https://github.com/nlnetlabs/unbound/commit/8d8fa4226613138f5a244a9f1a2506704e049180
Author: W.C.A. Wijngaards <wouter@nlnetlabs.nl>
Last-Update: 2026-08-18
