Description: CVE-2026-41292 - Limit number of parsed incoming EDNS options
 Parsing a long list of incoming EDNS options degrades performance,
 allowing a remote attacker to cause a denial of service by sending a
 packet whose OPT record RDATA contains a very large number of EDNS
 options. Each option triggers per-option processing (allocation and
 append), so an attacker-controlled long list results in excessive CPU
 and memory use.
 .
 This patch caps the number of EDNS options parsed from a single message
 at MAX_PARSED_EDNS_OPTIONS (100) by adding a counter to the option
 parsing loop. In unbound 1.13.1 the single parse_edns_options() helper
 is used for both the query and the response parse paths (the upstream
 fix later split it into parse_edns_options_from_query() and
 parse_extract_edns_from_response_msg()), so guarding this one loop
 covers both vulnerable paths.
Origin: backport, https://github.com/NLnetLabs/unbound/commit/ef5ca84360934fa1e857ebc371d4b093aea6355d
Author: Wouter Wijngaards <wouter@nlnetlabs.nl>
Last-Update: 2026-08-18
---
--- a/util/data/msgparse.c
+++ b/util/data/msgparse.c
@@ -48,6 +48,8 @@
 #include "sldns/parseutil.h"
 #include "sldns/wire2str.h"
 
+#define MAX_PARSED_EDNS_OPTIONS 100
+
 /** smart comparison of (compressed, valid) dnames from packet */
 static int
 smart_compare(sldns_buffer* pkt, uint8_t* dnow, 
@@ -943,9 +945,10 @@
 parse_edns_options(uint8_t* rdata_ptr, size_t rdata_len,
 	struct edns_data* edns, struct regional* region)
 {
+	int i = 0;
 	/* while still more options, and have code+len to read */
 	/* ignores partial content (i.e. rdata len 3) */
-	while(rdata_len >= 4) {
+	while(rdata_len >= 4 && i < MAX_PARSED_EDNS_OPTIONS) {
 		uint16_t opt_code = sldns_read_uint16(rdata_ptr);
 		uint16_t opt_len = sldns_read_uint16(rdata_ptr+2);
 		rdata_ptr += 4;
@@ -959,6 +962,7 @@
 		}
 		rdata_ptr += opt_len;
 		rdata_len -= opt_len;
+		i++;
 	}
 	return 1;
 }
Description: CVE-2026-42944 - Heap overflow and crash with multiple nsid, cookie, padding EDNS options
 Multiple (or large) nsid, cookie and padding EDNS options in a query could
 cause unbound to under-reserve buffer space and write EDNS option data past
 the end of the reply buffer, resulting in a heap overflow and crash.
 .
 This is an architecture-scoped backport of upstream commit
 fe946ba4e935a1528e6866e108e5bc9e7533ae18 to the 1.13.1 code base shipped in
 Debian bullseye.  The upstream commit also touches EDE handling, EDNS cookie
 parsing and the parse-time option de-duplication in
 parse_edns_options_from_query(); none of that code exists in 1.13.1, so those
 hunks are not applicable and are intentionally omitted.  The applicable
 root-cause fixes that this backport carries over are:
 .
   * calc_edns_field_size() accumulates option sizes in a size_t but returned a
     uint16_t.  With enough / large options the returned size wrapped past
     65535, so the caller reserved too little space in the packet buffer.  The
     return type (and its prototype) is widened to size_t so the reservation is
     always correct.
   * attach_edns_record_max_msg_sz() now bounds-checks every option (and the
     padding option) against both max_msg_sz and the actual buffer capacity
     before writing, so it stops instead of overflowing when the options do not
     fit.
 .
 A replacement regression test (edns_encode_overflow_test) is added to
 testcode/unitmain.c because the upstream EDE-based tests do not exist in this
 branch.  It builds an EDNS option list whose combined rdata exceeds 65535
 octets and asserts that calc_edns_field_size() reports the full, untruncated
 size (> 65535), which fails against the pre-fix uint16_t return value.
Origin: backport, https://github.com/NLnetLabs/unbound/commit/fe946ba4e935a1528e6866e108e5bc9e7533ae18
Author: W.C.A. Wijngaards <wouter@nlnetlabs.nl>
Last-Update: 2026-08-18
